claim: Session lifetime leak: The old destructor cleaned up m_pendingSession for cases where the queued lambda was dropped with `this`. The new design captures session directly in the lambda, and when the bridge is destroyed before the lambda runs, Qt drops the lambda but leaves the session leaked. The cleanup code was removed without providing an alternative leak prevention mechanism.; file: app/qttunebridge.cpp; quote: if (qttune_session_t* pending = m_pendingSession.exchange(nullptr)) {
        qttune_session_destroy(pending);
    }

claim: Session started on worker thread violates the original design invariant: The old comment explicitly stated 'until the GUI thread has taken the session, no worker may call onFrame with `this`'. Starting the session on the worker thread means frames can arrive before the GUI thread is ready, and the onFrame callback could race with bridge destruction. The cleanup code removal assumes this is safe, but onFrame may still be active if the session started successfully.; file: app/qttunebridge.cpp; quote: rc = qttune_session_start(session);
    if (rc != QT_OK) {
        qttune_unregister_frame_callback(session, &QtTuneBridge::onFrame, this);
        qttune_session_destroy(session);
        return QString::fromUtf8(qttune_status_string(rc));
    }

